# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

/* CROI_SYS_TASK_KILL */
int64_t task_kill(uint32_t task);
/* CROI_SYS_TASK_SUSPEND */
int64_t task_suspend(uint32_t task, uint32_t *token);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A task is a thread, a process or a job.

## task_kill

`task_kill` ends <task>, which needs DESTROY.
A handle of any other type is `CROI_ERR_WRONG_TYPE`.
The call returns when the kill is under way, not when it is done; the task's handle asserts `CROI_SIGNAL_TASK_TERMINATED` once it is.

Killing a thread marks it: an interruptible wait it is in ends, and the thread exits on its way back to user mode.
Killing a process kills each of its threads, and the process has the return code `CROI_TASK_RETCODE_SYSCALL_KILL` unless it already had one.
A process that never started is torn down at once.
Killing a job kills its processes and child jobs, recursively; the job can make no more.
The return codes of processes the kernel ended itself are `CROI_TASK_RETCODE_POLICY_KILL` (a job's policy) and `CROI_TASK_RETCODE_EXCEPTION_KILL` (an exception nothing handled).

## task_suspend

`task_suspend` stops <task>, a thread or a process, and writes to <token> a handle to a suspend token.
<task> needs WRITE.
The token has TRANSFER and INSPECT, and the task runs again when the token's last handle is closed.
Suspensions count: a task suspended twice needs both tokens closed.
Suspending a process suspends each of its threads once, and threads started afterwards start suspended.

A suspended thread parks on its way back to user mode, with its registers in a frame that thread_read_state(2) and thread_write_state(2) reach, and asserts `CROI_THREAD_SUSPENDED`; it asserts `CROI_THREAD_RUNNING` otherwise.
A thread blocked in port_wait(2) or futex_wait(2) is woken, and the call is made again when the thread resumes.
A thread in another wait, such as channel_call(2), parks when the wait ends.

The caller's own thread or process is `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`.
A thread that has ended, or a process that is not running or not yet started, is `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE`; any other type of handle is `CROI_ERR_WRONG_TYPE`.

